# .paraspace/Parafile — per-project config for para. Sourced as bash: it sets the
# few PARA_* variables para itself reads (version, image, routes). Everything else
# is a hook (.paraspace/hooks/). A real env var overrides any of these.
#
# This is the BAREST template: it installs nothing and boots nothing. It stands a
# Void Linux box up, gives you a shell (`para sh <name>`), and marks — in comments
# throughout — where you'd add your toolchain, clone, and app. Grow it into
# void-docker-gh (a runnable docker demo) as your project takes shape.
#
# Active below: only the keys a project actually has to decide. Everything para
# defaults sensibly is listed commented-out at the bottom.
#
# Full schema: the ParaSpace docs — docs/parafile.md in the package.

# para contract version these hooks target. para refuses (clearly) on a mismatch.
: "${PARA_CONTRACT:=1}"

# Project identity — the key para uses for ownership, `para ls` scoping, and the
# shared-volume name (para-home-<PARA_PROJECT>). It DEFAULTS to this directory's
# name, which is usually what you want, so it is left unset here. Declare it to
# pin an identity that survives renaming the checkout:
# : "${PARA_PROJECT:=my-project}"

# What `para image build` builds FROM: the incus image its builder launches, and
# one `sh -c` line run inside it before .paraspace/hooks/image-build. NEITHER has a
# default — para never picks your distro, and a para update must not be able to
# change it under you. Any incus image works (`images:debian/13`,
# `images:alpine/edge`, …); this template targets Void. The bootstrap must leave
# BASH in the builder (para runs the image-build hook with bash); here it also
# refreshes Void's bundled xbps, a rolling-release snapshot that can be too stale
# to install against. Set it to "" if your base needs nothing.
: "${PARA_IMAGE_BASE:=images:voidlinux}"
: "${PARA_IMAGE_BOOTSTRAP:=xbps-install -Syu xbps bash}"

# HTTP routes para publishes through its Caddy — one TLS site per "[sub:]port",
# comma-separated. This box boots nothing, so it declares NO routes — spelled
# out rather than left off, so it reads as a decision. `para up` still gives
# you a full box and `para sh <name>`; it just publishes no site, and `para ls`
# shows no URL. Once you run something, list its port here — a bare port is the
# workspace apex:
#   PARA_ROUTES="8080"              # -> https://<name>.$PARA_DOMAIN
#   PARA_ROUTES="3000,api:3001"     # several: commas, spaces, tabs or newlines
PARA_ROUTES="${PARA_ROUTES-}"

# ---- optional -----------------------------------------------------------------
# para's own defaults, spelled out so you don't have to go look them up. Uncomment
# only what you actually want to change.

# Git URL para clones into each workspace. This barebones template clones NOTHING
# (its provision hook has no clone step), so it's left unset. Point it at your repo
# and add a clone step to hooks/provision — see templates/void-docker-gh — once you
# have an app.
# : "${PARA_ORIGIN:=git@github.com:you/your-repo.git}"

# Directory under ~ to clone into (once you add a clone step). Default: app
# : "${PARA_CLONE_DIR:=your-repo}"

# Image `para up` launches, published by `para image build`. Defaults to
# $PARA_PROJECT — set it only to point several projects at one shared image.
# : "${PARA_IMAGE:=$PARA_PROJECT}"

# Wildcard domain the workspaces live under: https://<name>.$PARA_DOMAIN. Its
# wildcard must resolve to 127.0.0.1 — the default, paraspace.dev, already does.
# : "${PARA_DOMAIN:=paraspace.dev}"

# Shared-volume name — one authenticated home per project by default. Pin several
# projects to a single name to share dotfiles and auth across them.
# : "${PARA_VOLUME:=para-home-$PARA_PROJECT}"
